The source notes that provider reimbursement rates hadn't increased since 2011.

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:26.640
Wow.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:31.280
There were documented reports of doctor visits reimbursing for as low as $10.

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:33.280
$10 to see a medical professional.

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:34.280
Wait, really?

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:35.280
$10?

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:36.280
That's absurd.

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:39.440
You can't even cover the overhead of a clinic on a $10 reimbursement let alone

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:40.600
make a living.

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:41.600
Exactly.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:46.360
So what happens, the doctors pack up and move to Florida or New York where they can

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:47.560
actually get paid.

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:48.560
Right.

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:53.400
And if the hospitals close and the doctors leave, the entire local workforce gets sick

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:54.960
with no access to care.

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:57.200
Oh, I see the domino effect now.

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:58.200
Right.

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:01.560
If the workforce is sick, the manufacturers can't run their factories, the shipping

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:05.640
groups have no one to load the cargo, and businesses just can't operate.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:06.640
Wow.

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:10.480
The private sector realized that healthcare stability was intrinsically tied to

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:12.400
a workforce retention.

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:16.920
If the healthcare system spirals, the entire economy of the island spirals write down

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:17.920
with it.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:18.920
Okay.

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:19.920
That makes total sense.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:20.920
They saw the writing on the wall.

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:24.480
But getting the money authorized on paper in Washington is only step one.

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:27.080
The law had strict federal conditions attached to it, right?

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:28.400
Very strict conditions.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:33.240
To unlock an extra $200 million per year that was baked into the new law, Puerto

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:36.400
Rico had to prove to the federal government that they were actually reforming their

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:37.400
system.

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:38.400
Prove it how?

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:44.360
For example, they had to legally certify a physician payment floor of 70% of Medicare

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:45.920
Part B rates.

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:47.640
Let me get this straight.

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:51.240
Medicaid and Medicare are two totally completely different programs.

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:55.440
Why is the federal government forcing a Medicaid program to use Medicare rates as a

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:56.440
benchmark?

00:14:56.440 --> 00:15:00.720
Well, because Medicare rates are federally standardized and generally much more

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:01.720
stable.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:02.720
Okay.

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:06.720
By forcing the territory's Medicaid system to pay doctors at least 70% of what

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:10.600
Medicare pays for the same outpatient services, the federal government was

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:13.640
trying to end those absurd $10 reimbursements.

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:15.880
Oh, so it was like a failsafe.

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:16.880
Exactly.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:19.920
There's a structural guardrail to keep doctors from leaving the island.

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:23.400
They also mandated that Puerto Rico establish a program integrity lead within

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.240
six months to combat fraud.

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:27.360
And how did the private sector actually force this to happen?

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:30.360
I mean, you have a coalition of private businesses.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:34.040
How do they compel a slow-moving territorial government to suddenly

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:38.800
establish anti-fraud leads and rewrite doctor reimbursement codes in a matter of

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:39.800
weeks?

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:43.320
They did it by treating the Medicaid statute like a corporate compliance mandate

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:46.520
that had to be executed flawlessly just to keep the lights on.

00:15:46.520 --> 00:15:47.520
Interesting.

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:51.280
After the bill was signed, there was no victory lap.

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:56.440
In January 2020, just weeks after the ink tried, the Chamber of Commerce convened

00:15:56.440 --> 00:15:59.520
its health committee via these massive teleconferences.

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:01.680
Just imagine the logistics of that.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:05.120
Including competing hospital system executives, pharmaceutical reps and

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:09.200
shipping magnates all on a conference call during the holidays to execute

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:10.440
government policy.

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:13.760
It was a totally unprecedented mobilization.

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:16.560
They brought in the MCOs, the managed care organization.

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.400
These are the private insurance companies, right?

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:19.400
Yes.

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:22.200
Essentially, the companies the government contracts with to actually

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.440
administer the Medicaid plans on the ground.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:28.440
The coalition circulated strict action items.

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:31.120
They issued compulsory notices to health care providers to

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:32.920
enroll in the new systems.

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:36.560
They essentially built the entire implementation file themselves, gathering

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:40.040
the data, the hospital rate evidence and the compliance proof.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:41.480
They just did it themselves.

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:45.440
They handed this completed file to the federal government, specifically the

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:49.440
Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, to prove that Puerto Rico was

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:51.400
meeting these strict new conditions.

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:54.200
They literally did the government's homework for them.

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:57.960
They forced the bureaucratic machinery to turn so they could unlock that

00:16:57.960 --> 00:16:59.840
extra $200 million.

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:00.840
Precisely.

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:04.480
And the execution of that file is absolutely crucial because the sheer

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:08.480
amount of work they did to meet those conditions didn't just save them in

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:09.480
2020.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:10.480
No.

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:13.640
It laid the groundwork for the next generation of health care on the

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:14.640
island.

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:18.240
Which brings us to the legacy of this whole ordeal because we've seen how

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:21.960
the 2019 crisis was resolved by the skin of their teeth with a

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:26.200
dramatic presidential intervention and the private sector dragging the local

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:28.640
government across the finish line.

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:31.320
But what does this mean for the larger picture?

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:34.560
How do the chaotic policies forge over that one weekend to become the

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:35.960
permanent blueprint?

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:40.080
Well, if we connect this to the bigger picture, to see the legacy, we have

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:43.680
to fast forward three years to December 2022.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:46.160
The 2019 bill only bought them two years.

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:50.080
The pandemic provided some temporary relief through other emergency funds,

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:52.760
but eventually that prepaid card was flashing zero again.

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:53.760
Right.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:54.760
Back to the cliff.

00:17:54.760 --> 00:17:57.760
So in late 2022, President Biden signed the federal spending package

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:01.280
known as the Consolidated Appropriations Act of 2023.

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:05.360
So a new administration takes over, facing the exact same structural

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:06.920
cliff.

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:09.520
Do they finally fix the baseline problem?

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:12.720
Do they finally give Puerto Rico the same open-ended entitlement

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:14.560
as Ohio or California?

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:15.640
No, they don't.

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:18.720
And this is a core definitive finding in the source material.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:22.360
The 2023 Biden legislation did not invent a new health care system

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:23.520
for Puerto Rico.

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:25.960
It merely took the architecture that President Trump set into

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:28.240
law in 2019 and scaled it up.

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:31.520
So the DNA of the 2019 bill survived completely intact?

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:32.200
Completely.

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:33.080
Let's look at the elements.

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:35.600
In 2019, the emergency horizon was two years.

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:37.760
In 2023, they extended it to five years.

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:38.280
OK.

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:41.520
In 2019, the matching rate was locked at 76%.

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:46.240
The 2023 law extended that exact same 76% rate through 2027.

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:48.920
And what about those strict compliance conditions the private

00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:50.960
sector worked so hard to implement?

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:53.680
Like the physician payment floors tied to Medicare?

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:55.360
Those were kept and scaled up, too.

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:56.480
Really?

00:18:56.480 --> 00:18:59.920
The 2019 law required a 70% physician payment

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:03.280
floor to unlock $200 million.

00:19:03.280 --> 00:19:06.680
The 2023 law bumped that requirement up to a 75%

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:09.960
floor, which now unlocks $300 million a year.

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:11.200
Wow.

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:13.360
And the requirement for a program integrity lead

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:15.800
was not only kept, it was actually expanded

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:17.960
to include a contracting lead.

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:19.800
That is honestly fascinating.

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:23.120
A short term, heavily compromised two-year emergency patch

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:25.640
bored out of a weekend of political friction

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:28.040
and explicit threats of cutting funding

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:31.080
entirely accidentally became the foundational DNA

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:33.120
for the next decade of Puerto Rican health care.

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:34.120
Yeah, it really did.

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:36.400
But I have to ask, why?

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:39.720
Why does Washington love these duct tape solutions?

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:42.280
Why take a 2019 emergency patch and say, all right,

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:44.640
let's just make this the actual system but bigger

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:46.400
instead of fixing the root problem?

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:48.680
Because in Washington, the path of least resistance

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:50.320
is usually paved with precedent.

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:51.320
Precedent, right.

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:54.640
The private sector file from 2019-20,

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:56.680
their letters, their hospital rate evidence,

00:19:56.680 --> 00:19:59.640
their successful implementation of the physician floor,

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:02.640
it all proved that the duct tape actually held.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:04.200
Oh, so they knew it worked.

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:05.200
Exactly.

00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:08.320
It was the exact same evidence used by lawmakers

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:10.880
to justify scaling up the floor and the add-on money

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:12.240
in 2023.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:15.680
The Biden administration signed the five-year extension,

00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:18.480
but it was the Trump administration's signature in 2019

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:21.160
that wrote the base rules into the statute.

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:23.280
It was just easier to expand a proven patch

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:25.400
than to rewrite the whole Social Security Act.

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:27.840
And there's one more highly technical detail in the text

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:31.760
about this architecture, something called flush language.

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:33.520
What exactly is that and why does it matter?

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:35.160
Oh, the flush language.

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:37.680
That is perhaps the most vital and honestly most concerning

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:39.320
piece of the 2019 law.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:40.440
Why is it concerning?

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:43.360
It's a specific legal clause that explicitly directed

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:45.840
that once the temporary two-year funding expired,

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:48.400
the baseline caps would legally revert back

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:50.360
as if those increases had never existed.

00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:51.200
Wait, what?

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:53.520
Yeah, they would legally reset to the incredibly low

00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:55.880
pre-2019 baseline levels.

00:20:55.880 --> 00:20:56.640
Oh, wow.

00:20:56.640 --> 00:20:58.720
So they didn't just fail to fix the cliff.

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:01.760
They legally hardwired the cliff right back into the system.

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:02.480
They did.

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:05.520
They practically guaranteed that the crisis would repeat.

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:10.120
And the 2023 law included the exact same flush language

00:21:10.120 --> 00:21:13.800
for when the current five-year patch sunsets in 2027.

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:15.400
So what does this all mean for you?

00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:16.920
If you are listening to this right now,

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:20.160
you might be asking how a very specific territorial

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:23.520
funding drama from years ago applies to the wider world

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:25.080
of policy and business.

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:26.920
Right, there are broader lessons here.

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:28.960
Let's synthesize the ultimate lessons

00:21:28.960 --> 00:21:32.000
for the three key groups at the heart of this story.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:33.320
Let's start with the government.

00:21:33.320 --> 00:21:36.320
For the government, both federal and local,

00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:39.280
the lesson is that the pattern is completely fixed.

00:21:39.280 --> 00:21:41.680
They must always be prepared for the inevitable cliff

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:42.720
negotiations.

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:43.920
It's just baked in now.

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:44.720
Exactly.

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:46.920
Washington only moves the massive amounts of money

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:50.160
required when three specific things align,

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:52.640
an imminent undeniable financial cliff,

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:55.240
an organized integrity file proving the local government

00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:58.200
isn't wasting the funds, and a congressional leadership

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:00.840
deal that somehow survives White House scrutiny.

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:03.360
That exact sequence will have to repeat

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:07.080
before September 30, 2027, or the system crashes.

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:09.000
And what about the private sector?

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:11.160
The shipping magnates, the local pharmacies,

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:12.920
the manufacturers?

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:15.880
For the private sector, their 2019 playbook

00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:18.840
remains their permanent blueprint for survival.

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:19.440
Yeah.

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:22.080
They learn the hard way that treating Medicaid

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:24.400
is an economic bedrock and taking

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:27.400
on the heavy burden of immediate operational compliance

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:30.880
themselves is the only way to guarantee their economy

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:32.000
doesn't collapse.

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:33.280
They can't just wait for help.

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:35.520
No, they simply cannot rely on the government

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:36.440
to do it for them.

00:22:36.440 --> 00:22:39.280
If they want stability, they have to engineer it themselves.

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:41.600
Which brings us to the most important group,

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:43.240
the beneficiaries.

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:45.840
The millions of people who actually rely on these clinics

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:49.120
these doctors and these prescriptions just to survive.

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:51.200
For the beneficiaries, they remain caught

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:53.040
in a deeply tragic paradox.

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:53.880
Yeah.

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:55.520
Their fundamental health care stability

00:22:55.520 --> 00:22:58.200
is entirely dependent on down-to-the-wire Washington

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:00.960
drama, temporary supplements, and unpredictable

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:03.720
political whims, rather than a stable, permanent

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:06.280
infrastructure like the rest of the country enjoys.

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:08.800
It's a terrifying way to run a health care system.

00:23:08.800 --> 00:23:11.280
You're basically building a hospital on a fault line

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:13.040
and just hoping the earthquake holds off

00:23:13.040 --> 00:23:14.240
for another few years.

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:15.000
It is.

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:18.360
And this raises a broader critical question, something

00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:20.800
I'd leave you to mull over after we finish today's deep dive.

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:21.480
What's that?

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:24.880
If an entire territory's health care infrastructure

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:27.480
relies on last-minute emergency patches

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:29.960
and requires private businesses to do the government's

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:33.000
heavy lifting just to keep local clinics open,

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:36.400
what does that reveal about the fragility of how we fund

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:39.280
health care for marginalized populations at large?

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:43.080
That is a heavy but deeply necessary question to ponder.

00:23:43.120 --> 00:23:45.720
Because when the structural foundation is built on duct tape

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:49.320
and temporary promises, you are always just one decisive weekend

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:50.800
away from total collapse.

